{"kind":"request","text":"claude: make the fold name the performer as the waiting party on awaiting-merge commitments, so the board and the lanes stop showing approved work as owned by nobody. Hugh reported the symptom today (2026-09-01): the board UI shows many \"awaiting merge / unassigned\" rows in both the \"open\" and \"reasoning moved\" tabs. I investigated and confirmed the mechanism at the live internal/workroom and internal/statusview artifacts' commit 5a89dcaf7d0558591b5740c171d7902d09385dd7 (identical files at current main).\n\nCONFIRMED MECHANISM. projectCommitments sets WaitingOn = \"\" on every awaiting-merge commitment, on both the direct-completion path (internal/workroom/fold.go:3208-3209) and the promised path (fold.go:3253-3254). The comment there correctly rules out naming the REQUESTER — an artifact has a satisfier of none, so the requester cannot ratify it — but then names nobody, although a truthful waiting party exists: the PERFORMER, who must sign the merge of their approved head. Downstream, the board UI renders the empty waiting_on on a promised commitment as \"unassigned\" (ui/src/lib/rows.ts:202 and :210), and lane classification keys on WaitingOn (internal/statusview/query.go:225 and the mirrored branch in BuildActorStatus), so an awaiting-merge row appears in the requester's you_are_waiting_on lane but in NOBODY's waiting_on_you lane. That blind spot is why merges pile up unnoticed: 15 awaiting-merge commitments, 9 of them stale, currently sit on the board with no queue showing them. Appearing in both tabs is by design (\"reasoning moved\" is the stale subset of open, rows.ts:89) and is NOT in scope; the defect is only the missing waiting party.\n\nDIRECTION. Set WaitingOn to the performer at both fold sites: promiseRecord.record.Actor on the promised path, completion.record.Actor on the direct-completion path. Update the fold.go comment so it no longer claims no waiting party can be named. No UI change should be needed — rows.ts already renders waiting_on when present — but verify the board row now says the performer's name and correct the UI only if it contradicts the fold. Note the satisfied/superseded/cancelled/reneged branches already clear WaitingOn after those transitions; keep that behaviour.\n\nCONDITIONS: (1) a regression proves an awaiting-merge commitment (both a promised one and a direct completion) projects WaitingOn = the performer, and that the row is classified into the performer's waiting_on_you lane by both gs work and gs status; the projection assertions fail against the current code. (2) Existing workroom, statusview, and UI gates stay green at the exact head (run make ui / npm ci as the UI gates need; the ui-check is not in the Go gates). (3) Implement on a request/\u003cslug\u003e worktree with Rests-On per discipline, publish exact-head artifacts for internal/workroom and internal/statusview (and the UI path only if the UI changed), and request independent review citing this request. (4) Any documentation page that states the awaiting-merge waiting-party contract is updated in the same head.","body":{"conditions":"Regression proves awaiting-merge commitments (promised and direct-completion) project WaitingOn = performer and classify into the performer's waiting_on_you lane in both gs work and gs status, failing before the fix; existing workroom/statusview/UI gates green at the exact head; exact-head artifacts published for internal/workroom and internal/statusview (plus UI path only if changed) and independent review requested citing this request; docs stating the awaiting-merge waiting-party contract updated in the same head.","to":"a5d35aa7e4799472a208663d7f024917442bd513929f9eccf450628ccf70095d"}}